<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ove Gjedde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1594">1594</a>–<a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ove Gjedde was a Danish naval officer who led the first expedition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-east-india-company">Danish East India Company</a> to Asia, sailing in 1618. After visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> he negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghunatha-nayak">Raghunatha Nayak</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, who in 1620 granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a>, where the fort of Dansborg was built. He later served as Admiral of the Realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ove-gjedde.jpg" alt="Ove Gjedde" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karel van Mander III · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karel_van_III_Mander_-_Rigsadmiral_Ove_Gjedde_-_KMS605_-_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ove Gjedde was born on 27 December 1594 at Tomarps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, the fourth son of Brostrup Gjedde and Dorthe Ulfeldt. He studied at Sorø Academy from 1609 and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, taking degrees in fortification and law, and served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> army at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Braunschweig</a> in 1615. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> he worked as a secretary in the Danish Chancellery, and his business knowledge and loyalty to King Christian IV won him the leadership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-east-india-company">Danish East India Company</a>'s first expedition, though he had little acquaintance with the navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The fleet of over 400 men left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> on 29 November 1618 for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, which had promised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> privileges if they drove the Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. Off Cape Verde Gjedde captured two French privateering vessels, an attack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> cost 12 men, and scurvy struck the crews. When the fleet reached Ceylon in May 1620, Senarat of Kandy had already made peace with the Portuguese, but under Gjedde's threats he signed a treaty on 21 August 1620 ceding the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a>. With the help of the Dutchman Roland Crappé, Gjedde then secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> and built a fort of his own design.</p><p>Many regarded the expedition as a failure, as the treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> proved ineffectual. From 1622 Gjedde held Norwegian fiefs, Brunla and Numedal, later Tønsberg and Bratsberg, and became inspector and then director of the Kongsberg Silver Mines, in which he held a quarter share with Christian IV and Jens Hermansson Juel. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torstenson-war">Torstenson War</a> he commanded a Norwegian fleet in 1644, but his ship, the St. Sophia, ran onto a rock while blockading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>. After the war he became Admiral of the Realm and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsradet">Council of the Realm</a>, possibly through his connection with Hannibal Sehested.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Christian IV's death in 1648 he sat in the interim government that drafted a highly restrictive charter for Frederick III. Accused of irregularities in the war accounts, though never convicted, he was given no command in the Dano-Swedish War of 1657–1658. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> broke the peace in 1658, Gjedde was captured by the Swedes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> and held until 1660; he died at Sokkelund on 19 December 1660. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludvig-holberg">Ludvig Holberg</a> credited him with bringing the habit of drinking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ove Gjedde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Danish expedition under <b>Ove Gjedde</b> reached south India, and in 1620 Raghunatha Nayak of Thanjavur granted the Danish East India Company the village of Tranquebar (Tharangambadi).</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-india">Danish India</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tomarps Kungsgård Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.15,13.06&amp;year=1636" aria-label="Tomarps Kungsgård Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4834 252 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ove Gjedde"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5284 522h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5284 522h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.15,13.06&amp;year=1636">What was here in 1636? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
